Here's how to structure a block decorator for this storefront.

## The shape

Every block is two files in `components/eds-storefront/blocks/blockname/`: `blockname.js` and `blockname.css`. The JS exports one default async function that AEM calls automatically:

```javascript
export default async function decorate(block) {
  // 1. Guard against empty content
  if (!block.children.length) return;

  // 2. Read config / variants
  const isCompact = block.classList.contains('compact');

  // 3. Extract data from the rows AEM gives you
  const rows = [...block.children];

  // 4. Rebuild the DOM
  const container = document.createElement('div');
  container.classList.add('blockname-list');
  rows.forEach((row) => {
    const cells = [...row.children];
    const title = cells[0]?.textContent.trim();
    if (!title) return;                      // skip malformed rows
    const img = cells[1]?.querySelector('picture'); // move it, don't clone-and-lose
    const item = document.createElement('div');
    if (img) item.append(img);
    const h3 = document.createElement('h3');
    h3.textContent = title;
    item.append(h3);
    container.append(item);
  });
  block.textContent = '';
  block.append(container);

  // 5. Wire up events (delegation for dynamic content)
  block.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const btn = e.target.closest('button');
    if (btn) { /* handle */ }
  });
}
```

The input is always the same: each authored table row arrives as a `<div>`, each cell as a nested `<div>`. Your job is to read those, then replace them with the markup you actually want.

## Rules that matter on this project

- **Inspect the real HTML first.** `curl <preview-url>/page.plain.html` or `console.log(block.outerHTML)`. Don't guess the cell layout.
- **Check `/blocks` for an existing block before writing a new one.** If something is 80% there, adapt it. If you build from scratch, note why.
- **Drop-in work is a hard handoff.** If the block renders a drop-in container, uses slots, the event bus, or `@dropins/*/api.js`, that part goes through the Drop-in Developer skill — the block decorator only owns the surrounding structure.
- **CSS: every selector starts with `.blockname`**, no exceptions. And this storefront has a typography rule: use `font: var(--type-…)` from `styles/styles.css` for all text — never invent a `font-size`.
- **Be defensive.** Use `?.` on every cell access and skip rows missing required fields; authors will omit things.
- **Async work**: if you fetch data, wrap it in try/catch and toggle a `loading` class so there's a visible state.

## Workflow

Inspect HTML → write decorator → scoped CSS (mobile-first, breakpoints at 600/900/1200px) → test locally at `http://localhost:3000` with a test file in `drafts/agent/` (copy the script tags from `head.html` or your JS never runs) → then `sync_storefront` to push.

If you tell me what the block should do, I can check the existing blocks for a starting point and sketch the actual decorator.
